 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Earlier that morning, in the Claude Monet kitchen, Max turns up late again and Chef Barinov is waiting for him. Then Fedya, another cook, starts needling Max about his cooking, and it ends in a bet. Listen in Russian first; the odd missed word doesn't matter.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро, шеф!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок, ты постоянно опаздываешь на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, мне не выдали форму вовремя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хватит оправданий, быстро иди работать!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Ты так же плохо шутишь, как и готовишь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я не понял твоей шутки, Федя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Это обычное состояние для твоего мозга.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты хочешь сказать, что я плохо готовлю?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да, именно это я и говорю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А ты думаешь, ты готовишь лучше меня?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Уж точно намного лучше тебя!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Тогда давай устроим кулинарный спор!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Спорим, огузок! Я легко тебя обыграю!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Посмотрим, кто из нас лучший повар!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now line by line, from Max's greeting. Notice that nobody in this kitchen says anything nice.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе утро, шеф!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, Chef!' Literally 'kind morning'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Доброе",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ends in -oe to match ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "утро",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", a noun ending in -o. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "шеф",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is borrowed from French, and in this kitchen it simply literally translates to 'boss'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок, ты постоянно опаздываешь на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Runt, you are constantly late for work.' , Barinov's name for Max, is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Огузок",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " really the rump cut of beef. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "опаздываешь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a present-tense verb, and paired with ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "постоянно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'constantly', it describes a habit of being late.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, мне не выдали форму вовремя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, they did not issue my uniform on time.' Max says ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "мне",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "выдали",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", a phrase conveying 'to me they didn't issue', so nobody in particular gets the blame. The adverb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "вовремя",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", for 'on time', is written as one word.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хватит оправданий, быстро иди работать!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Enough excuses, quickly go to work!' Following ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хватит",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", indicating 'enough', the thing you've had enough of takes a genitive ending, as seen in ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "оправданий",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'excuses'. The command ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "иди",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "работать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " simply equates to 'go work', placing two verbs side by side.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Ты так же плохо шутишь, как и готовишь.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You joke as badly as you cook.' The two halves match: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "так",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "плохо",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " acts as 'just as badly', while ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "как",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "и",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " represents 'as also'. The verb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "готовишь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which expresses 'you cook', also carries the sense of 'you prepare'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я не понял твоей шутки, Федя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I didn't understand your joke, Fedya.' After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "понял",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", the negative for 'understood', the joke becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "шутки",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", and the feminine pronoun for 'your' turns into ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "твоей",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". Notice that ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "понял",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", lacking an -a ending, tells you a man is speaking.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Это обычное состояние для твоего мозга.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is a normal state for your brain.' The preposition ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "для",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", expressing 'for', changes the case of what follows it: the base forms for 'your brain' become ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "твоего",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "мозга",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". It's an insult delivered almost calmly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты хочешь сказать, что я плохо готовлю?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do you want to say that I cook badly?' The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "хочешь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "сказать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " pairs 'you want' and 'to say', combining two verbs without a linking 'to'. The conjunction ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "что",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " functions here as 'that', though it also serves as the question word for 'what'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да, именно это я и говорю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, that is exactly what I say.' The adverb ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "именно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " carries the weight of 'exactly' or 'precisely'. The small ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "и",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " before ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "говорю",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " does not act as 'and' here: it just leans on the verb for emphasis.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А ты думаешь, ты готовишь лучше меня?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And do you think you cook better than me?' There is no direct equivalent for 'than'. Russian shifts 'me' into the form ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "меня",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and says ",
